288
00:09:27,468 --> 00:09:29,442
Long term bond funds can move a

289
00:09:29,442 --> 00:09:29,697
lot.

290
00:09:30,046 --> 00:09:33,144
Municipal bond funds can pay income that's

291
00:09:33,144 --> 00:09:35,284
free from federal income tax,

292
00:09:35,284 --> 00:09:37,349
and sometimes state tax too.

293
00:09:37,529 --> 00:09:39,568
They can be useful for people in

294
00:09:39,568 --> 00:09:42,754
higher tax brackets investing in taxable accounts.

295
00:09:43,103 --> 00:09:45,001
Bond funds earn their place in a

296
00:09:45,001 --> 00:09:47,492
portfolio by doing something stocks don't.

297
00:09:47,672 --> 00:09:49,621
In many stock market crashes,

298
00:09:49,621 --> 00:09:52,646
high quality government bonds have held their

299
00:09:52,646 --> 00:09:53,990
value or even risen,

300
00:09:53,990 --> 00:09:55,335
cushioning the fall.

301
00:09:55,515 --> 00:09:58,336
Not always, as 2022 showed,

302
00:09:58,336 --> 00:09:59,381
but often.

303
00:09:59,561 --> 00:10:01,847
That's why many retirees hold bond funds

304
00:10:01,847 --> 00:10:03,791
not just for their monthly income,

305
00:10:03,791 --> 00:10:05,734
but as a stabilizer that makes the

306
00:10:05,734 --> 00:10:07,792
whole portfolio easier to live with.

307
00:10:08,142 --> 00:10:08,909
Try this.

308
00:10:09,089 --> 00:10:10,687
If you own a bond fund,

309
00:10:10,687 --> 00:10:13,119
look up its duration and its credit

310
00:10:13,119 --> 00:10:13,675
quality.

311
00:10:13,855 --> 00:10:15,704
Those two numbers tell you most of

312
00:10:15,704 --> 00:10:17,391
what you need to know about its

313
00:10:17,391 --> 00:10:17,663
risk.

314
00:10:18,012 --> 00:10:19,069
That's angle one.

315
00:10:19,249 --> 00:10:20,838
High yield savings,

316
00:10:20,838 --> 00:10:22,428
money market funds,

317
00:10:22,428 --> 00:10:25,356
Treasury bills, CDs and bond funds.

318
00:10:25,536 --> 00:10:28,403
Safer assets with modest monthly income.

319
00:10:28,583 --> 00:10:29,698
Now angle two.

320
00:10:29,878 --> 00:10:32,513
Higher yields, and the psychology that gets

321
00:10:32,513 --> 00:10:33,616
people in trouble.

322
00:10:33,966 --> 00:10:35,154
As yields go up,

323
00:10:35,154 --> 00:10:36,119
so does risk,

324
00:10:36,119 --> 00:10:38,123
and so do the mental traps.

325
00:10:38,303 --> 00:10:40,252
Monthly income feels wonderful,

326
00:10:40,252 --> 00:10:42,579
and that feeling can push people into

327
00:10:42,579 --> 00:10:45,095
investments they don't fully understand.

328
00:10:45,275 --> 00:10:47,543
Here are five higher yield assets,

329
00:10:47,543 --> 00:10:49,744
each with a psychological lesson.

330
00:10:50,094 --> 00:10:51,464
Asset number six.

331
00:10:51,644 --> 00:10:54,419
Monthly dividend stocks and ETFs.

332
00:10:54,769 --> 00:10:55,512
Most U.

333
00:10:55,692 --> 00:10:56,041
S.

334
00:10:56,221 --> 00:10:59,072
companies that pay dividends pay them quarterly,

335
00:10:59,072 --> 00:11:00,319
but some pay monthly.

336
00:11:00,499 --> 00:11:03,402
One well known example is Realty Income,

337
00:11:03,402 --> 00:11:06,232
a real estate company that calls itself

338
00:11:06,232 --> 00:11:08,336
The Monthly Dividend Company.

339
00:11:08,516 --> 00:11:11,135
There are also ETFs that collect dividends

340
00:11:11,135 --> 00:11:13,380
from many companies and pay monthly.

341
00:11:13,730 --> 00:11:15,518
Here's the psychological trap.

342
00:11:15,698 --> 00:11:17,655
People love monthly payments,

343
00:11:17,655 --> 00:11:20,960
so they sometimes choose investments just because

344
00:11:20,960 --> 00:11:22,107
they pay monthly,

345
00:11:22,107 --> 00:11:24,872
even if they're more expensive or riskier

346
00:11:24,872 --> 00:11:26,356
than quarterly payers.

347
00:11:26,536 --> 00:11:29,259
The payment schedule doesn't change the total

348
00:11:29,259 --> 00:11:29,683
return.

349
00:11:30,032 --> 00:11:32,703
Remember also that dividends aren't free money.

350
00:11:32,883 --> 00:11:34,943
When a company pays a dividend,

351
00:11:34,943 --> 00:11:37,734
its share price typically drops by roughly

352
00:11:37,734 --> 00:11:40,259
the dividend amount on the ex dividend

353
00:11:40,259 --> 00:11:40,592
date.

354
00:11:40,772 --> 00:11:42,937
What matters is total return,

355
00:11:42,937 --> 00:11:45,102
dividends plus price changes.

356
00:11:45,452 --> 00:11:47,391
Dividends can also be cut.

357
00:11:47,571 --> 00:11:48,926
During tough times,

358
00:11:48,926 --> 00:11:52,564
many companies have reduced or suspended dividends.

359
00:11:52,744 --> 00:11:54,735
A dividend is a decision by the

360
00:11:54,735 --> 00:11:56,726
company's board, not a promise.

361
00:11:57,075 --> 00:11:59,444
There's also a concentration trap.

362
00:11:59,624 --> 00:12:02,332
Some people build a portfolio of only

363
00:12:02,332 --> 00:12:05,259
a handful of high yield dividend stocks,

364
00:12:05,259 --> 00:12:08,406
often clustered in the same few industries,

365
00:12:08,406 --> 00:12:11,187
like utilities, energy or real estate.

366
00:12:11,367 --> 00:12:12,882
If that sector struggles,

367
00:12:12,882 --> 00:12:14,942
much of their income can shrink at

368
00:12:14,942 --> 00:12:15,245
once.

369
00:12:15,425 --> 00:12:18,816
A diversified dividend fund spreads that risk

370
00:12:18,816 --> 00:12:21,605
across many companies and industries,

371
00:12:21,605 --> 00:12:24,017
so no single cut hurts too much.

372
00:12:24,366 --> 00:12:25,237
Try this.

373
00:12:25,417 --> 00:12:28,082
Before buying any dividend investment,

374
00:12:28,082 --> 00:12:30,397
look at its total return over ten

375
00:12:30,397 --> 00:12:32,221
years, not just its yield.

376
00:12:32,401 --> 00:12:34,869
A high yield with poor total return

377
00:12:34,869 --> 00:12:36,139
is a warning sign.

378
00:12:36,489 --> 00:12:37,801
Asset number seven.

379
00:12:37,981 --> 00:12:40,234
Real estate investment trusts.

380
00:12:40,583 --> 00:12:43,581
REITs are companies that own or finance

381
00:12:43,581 --> 00:12:45,811
income producing real estate,

382
00:12:45,811 --> 00:12:47,963
like apartments, warehouses,

383
00:12:47,963 --> 00:12:50,115
offices or shopping centers.

384
00:12:50,295 --> 00:12:52,444
By law, to keep their special tax

385
00:12:52,444 --> 00:12:55,568
status, REITs generally must distribute at least

386
00:12:55,568 --> 00:12:58,238
ninety percent of their taxable income to

387
00:12:58,238 --> 00:12:59,084
shareholders.

388
00:12:59,433 --> 00:13:01,782
That's why REITs often have higher yields

389
00:13:01,782 --> 00:13:02,928
than average stocks.

390
00:13:03,108 --> 00:13:04,313
Some pay monthly,

391
00:13:04,313 --> 00:13:07,220
and REIT index funds give you diversified

392
00:13:07,220 --> 00:13:09,772
exposure to many properties at once.

393
00:13:10,122 --> 00:13:13,228
The psychological trap with REITs is assuming

394
00:13:13,228 --> 00:13:15,092
real estate is always safe.

395
00:13:15,272 --> 00:13:17,223
REIT prices can fall sharply,

396
00:13:17,223 --> 00:13:19,914
especially when interest rates rise or a

397
00:13:19,914 --> 00:13:21,529
property type struggles.

398
00:13:21,709 --> 00:13:23,413
Office REITs, for example,

399
00:13:23,413 --> 00:13:26,493
faced serious challenges as remote work spread.

400
00:13:26,842 --> 00:13:30,064
REIT dividends are often taxed as ordinary

401
00:13:30,064 --> 00:13:33,592
income rather than at lower qualified dividend

402
00:13:33,592 --> 00:13:36,506
rates, so many investors hold REITs in

403
00:13:36,506 --> 00:13:39,114
tax advantaged accounts like IRAs.

404
00:13:39,464 --> 00:13:41,852
REITs also have a useful feature for

405
00:13:41,852 --> 00:13:43,377
monthly income seekers.

406
00:13:43,557 --> 00:13:45,068
They trade like stocks,

407
00:13:45,068 --> 00:13:46,843
so you can buy as little as

408
00:13:46,843 --> 00:13:48,814
one share, or even a fraction,

409
00:13:48,814 --> 00:13:50,523
and sell any business day.

410
00:13:50,703 --> 00:13:52,743
Compare that with a rental property,

411
00:13:52,743 --> 00:13:55,009
which might take months and thousands of

412
00:13:55,009 --> 00:13:56,369
dollars in fees to sell.

413
00:13:56,549 --> 00:13:57,731
For many people,

414
00:13:57,731 --> 00:14:00,612
that liquidity makes REITs a far easier

415
00:14:00,612 --> 00:14:02,976
way to add real estate income to

416
00:14:02,976 --> 00:14:03,863
a portfolio.

417
00:14:04,212 --> 00:14:05,072
Try this.

418
00:14:05,252 --> 00:14:07,756
If you want real estate income without

419
00:14:07,756 --> 00:14:08,876
being a landlord,

420
00:14:08,876 --> 00:14:11,116
compare a low cost REIT index fund

421
00:14:11,116 --> 00:14:12,566
with individual REITs.

422
00:14:12,746 --> 00:14:15,248
The index fund spreads your risk across

423
00:14:15,248 --> 00:14:16,531
many property types.

424
00:14:16,880 --> 00:14:18,123
Asset number eight.

425
00:14:18,303 --> 00:14:19,371
Rental property.

426
00:14:19,720 --> 00:14:22,671
Rental property is the classic monthly income

427
00:14:22,671 --> 00:14:23,064
asset.

428
00:14:23,244 --> 00:14:25,340
Tenants pay rent every month,

429
00:14:25,340 --> 00:14:28,304
and after expenses and mortgage payments,

430
00:14:28,304 --> 00:14:30,616
the leftover cash flow is yours.

431
00:14:30,796 --> 00:14:33,080
Over time, rents may rise and the

432
00:14:33,080 --> 00:14:34,256
mortgage shrinks.

433
00:14:34,606 --> 00:14:37,773
The psychological trap is focusing on gross

434
00:14:37,773 --> 00:14:39,982
rent instead of net cash flow.

435
00:14:40,162 --> 00:14:41,968
Property taxes, insurance,

436
00:14:41,968 --> 00:14:45,787
repairs, vacancies, maintenance and management fees can

437
00:14:45,787 --> 00:14:47,871
consume a large share of rent.

438
00:14:48,051 --> 00:14:50,839
Many new landlords discover their real profit

439
00:14:50,839 --> 00:14:53,008
is much smaller than they expected.

440
00:14:53,358 --> 00:14:55,953
Rental income is also not passive for

441
00:14:55,953 --> 00:14:56,795
most people.

442
00:14:56,975 --> 00:15:00,135
Finding tenants, handling repairs and dealing with

443
00:15:00,135 --> 00:15:01,398
problems takes time.

444
00:15:01,578 --> 00:15:03,606
Hiring a property manager helps,

445
00:15:03,606 --> 00:15:05,317
but it reduces your income.

446
00:15:05,666 --> 00:15:07,186
On the positive side,

447
00:15:07,186 --> 00:15:09,502
rental property offers leverage,

448
00:15:09,502 --> 00:15:13,700
potential appreciation and tax benefits like depreciation.

449
00:15:13,880 --> 00:15:15,247
For the right person,

450
00:15:15,247 --> 00:15:17,523
it can be a powerful monthly income

451
00:15:17,523 --> 00:15:17,979
source.

452
00:15:18,328 --> 00:15:20,070
Here's a rough illustration.

453
00:15:20,250 --> 00:15:22,813
A property renting for two thousand dollars

454
00:15:22,813 --> 00:15:24,720
a month might have a mortgage of

455
00:15:24,720 --> 00:15:26,210
one thousand two hundred,

456
00:15:26,210 --> 00:15:28,951
property taxes and insurance of three hundred,

457
00:15:28,951 --> 00:15:31,037
and a repair and vacancy reserve of

458
00:15:31,037 --> 00:15:31,871
three hundred.

459
00:15:32,051 --> 00:15:33,988
That leaves about two hundred dollars a

460
00:15:33,988 --> 00:15:34,931
month of cash flow.

461
00:15:35,111 --> 00:15:36,878
Not huge, but it can grow as

462
00:15:36,878 --> 00:15:39,465
rents rise and the mortgage is eventually

463
00:15:39,465 --> 00:15:40,033
paid off.

464
00:15:40,213 --> 00:15:43,267
The real wealth often comes over decades.

465
00:15:43,616 --> 00:15:44,348
Try this.

466
00:15:44,528 --> 00:15:46,024
Before buying a rental,

467
00:15:46,024 --> 00:15:49,016
estimate your net cash flow using conservative

468
00:15:49,016 --> 00:15:51,618
numbers, including at least one month of

469
00:15:51,618 --> 00:15:54,025
vacancy per year and a repair budget.

470
00:15:54,374 --> 00:15:55,338
Quick pause.

471
00:15:55,518 --> 00:15:57,719
If you're starting to picture your own

472
00:15:57,719 --> 00:15:59,052
monthly income machine,

473
00:15:59,052 --> 00:16:01,195
please subscribe to Ryan Rover Money.

474
00:16:01,375 --> 00:16:03,158
It's free, and it helps us keep

475
00:16:03,158 --> 00:16:03,906
making these.

476
00:16:04,086 --> 00:16:06,002
Now, asset number nine.

477
00:16:06,351 --> 00:16:07,559
Asset number nine.

478
00:16:07,739 --> 00:16:09,527
Covered call ETFs.

479
00:16:09,876 --> 00:16:12,737
Covered call ETFs own stocks and sell

480
00:16:12,737 --> 00:16:14,360
call options on them,

481
00:16:14,360 --> 00:16:16,448
collecting option premiums.

482
00:16:16,628 --> 00:16:19,501
Those premiums can create very high yields,

483
00:16:19,501 --> 00:16:20,771
often paid monthly,

484
00:16:20,771 --> 00:16:23,645
which makes these funds popular with income

485
00:16:23,645 --> 00:16:24,313
investors.

486
00:16:24,663 --> 00:16:25,673
Here's the catch.

487
00:16:25,853 --> 00:16:27,362
By selling call options,

488
00:16:27,362 --> 00:16:29,310
these funds give up much of the

489
00:16:29,310 --> 00:16:31,322
upside when stocks rise sharply.

490
00:16:31,502 --> 00:16:32,644
In strong markets,

491
00:16:32,644 --> 00:16:34,738
they can lag far behind a regular

492
00:16:34,738 --> 00:16:37,150
index fund, while still taking most of

493
00:16:37,150 --> 00:16:38,990
the losses when markets fall.

494
00:16:39,340 --> 00:16:42,138
The psychological trap is yield fixation.

495
00:16:42,318 --> 00:16:44,287
A big yield feels like a big

496
00:16:44,287 --> 00:16:44,780
return.

497
00:16:44,960 --> 00:16:47,555
But some covered call funds have delivered

498
00:16:47,555 --> 00:16:50,213
lower total returns than simple index funds

499
00:16:50,213 --> 00:16:52,623
over time, even with their high monthly

500
00:16:52,623 --> 00:16:53,180
payments.

501
00:16:53,529 --> 00:16:55,748
Part of a high distribution can also

502
00:16:55,748 --> 00:16:58,029
come from returning your own capital,

503
00:16:58,029 --> 00:16:59,508
rather than true income.

504
00:16:59,688 --> 00:17:01,651
That can slowly shrink your investment.

505
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,121
Covered call funds can still have a

506
00:17:04,121 --> 00:17:04,485
place.

507
00:17:04,665 --> 00:17:06,654
Some retirees value a smoother,

508
00:17:06,654 --> 00:17:09,670
higher monthly payout more than maximum growth,

509
00:17:09,670 --> 00:17:12,301
and accept lagging in strong bull markets

510
00:17:12,301 --> 00:17:13,071
in exchange.

511
00:17:13,251 --> 00:17:14,769
That's a reasonable choice,

512
00:17:14,769 --> 00:17:16,401
as long as it's a choice made

513
00:17:16,401 --> 00:17:17,244
with open eyes.

514
00:17:17,424 --> 00:17:19,497
The problem is only when people buy

515
00:17:19,497 --> 00:17:21,569
them believing a high yield means a

516
00:17:21,569 --> 00:17:23,345
high return with no trade off.

517
00:17:23,695 --> 00:17:24,520
Try this.

518
00:17:24,700 --> 00:17:27,151
Before buying a covered call fund,

519
00:17:27,151 --> 00:17:29,818
compare its total return with a plain

520
00:17:29,818 --> 00:17:32,269
index fund over five or ten years.

521
00:17:32,449 --> 00:17:35,236
Then decide whether the steady income is

522
00:17:35,236 --> 00:17:36,629
worth the trade off.

523
00:17:36,979 --> 00:17:38,233
Asset number ten.

524
00:17:38,413 --> 00:17:39,621
Income annuities.

525
00:17:39,970 --> 00:17:41,878
A simple income annuity,

526
00:17:41,878 --> 00:17:46,011
sometimes called a single premium immediate annuity,

527
00:17:46,011 --> 00:17:48,237
works like buying a pension.

528
00:17:48,417 --> 00:17:50,984
You give an insurance company a lump

529
00:17:50,984 --> 00:17:52,981
sum, and it pays you a fixed

530
00:17:52,981 --> 00:17:54,978
amount every month for life,

531
00:17:54,978 --> 00:17:56,404
or for a set period.

532
00:17:56,754 --> 00:17:59,803
The big benefit is protection against outliving

533
00:17:59,803 --> 00:18:00,516
your money.

534
00:18:00,696 --> 00:18:02,455
No matter how long you live,

535
00:18:02,455 --> 00:18:03,900
the checks keep coming,

536
00:18:03,900 --> 00:18:05,282
backed by the insurer.

537
00:18:05,462 --> 00:18:08,716
Economists have long noted that annuities can

538
00:18:08,716 --> 00:18:11,898
be valuable, yet relatively few retirees buy

539
00:18:11,898 --> 00:18:14,936
them, sometimes called the annuity puzzle.

540
00:18:15,285 --> 00:18:17,665
The psychological barrier is the fear of

541
00:18:17,665 --> 00:18:19,628
dying early and losing the money.

542
00:18:19,808 --> 00:18:20,996
That fear is real,

543
00:18:20,996 --> 00:18:24,031
but some annuities offer guaranteed periods or

544
00:18:24,031 --> 00:18:25,153
refunds to heirs,

545
00:18:25,153 --> 00:18:27,133
at the cost of lower payments.

546
00:18:27,483 --> 00:18:28,250
Be careful.

547
00:18:28,430 --> 00:18:31,210
Many complex annuities have high fees,

548
00:18:31,210 --> 00:18:33,991
surrender charges and confusing terms.

549
00:18:34,171 --> 00:18:36,839
Simple income annuities are usually easier to

550
00:18:36,839 --> 00:18:37,491
understand.

551
00:18:37,671 --> 00:18:40,886
Payments generally aren't inflation adjusted unless you

552
00:18:40,886 --> 00:18:42,113
pay for that feature,

553
00:18:42,113 --> 00:18:44,568
and they depend on the insurer's strength.

554
00:18:44,917 --> 00:18:47,506
Here's how annuity payments generally work.

555
00:18:47,686 --> 00:18:49,627
The older you are when you buy,

556
00:18:49,627 --> 00:18:51,568
the higher the monthly payment,

557
00:18:51,568 --> 00:18:53,948
because the insurer expects to pay for

558
00:18:53,948 --> 00:18:54,699
fewer years.

559
00:18:54,879 --> 00:18:57,341
Payments also depend on interest rates at

560
00:18:57,341 --> 00:18:58,362
the time you buy.

561
00:18:58,542 --> 00:19:01,164
That's why some retirees buy annuities in

562
00:19:01,164 --> 00:19:02,827
stages over several years,

563
00:19:02,827 --> 00:19:04,362
rather than all at once,

564
00:19:04,362 --> 00:19:06,088
to spread out their timing.

565
00:19:06,438 --> 00:19:07,332
Try this.

566
00:19:07,512 --> 00:19:09,214
If you're near retirement,

567
00:19:09,214 --> 00:19:11,833
ask a fee only fiduciary planner whether

568
00:19:11,833 --> 00:19:15,040
covering basic expenses with Social Security plus

569
00:19:15,040 --> 00:19:17,462
a simple annuity makes sense for you.

570
00:19:17,812 --> 00:19:18,915
That's angle two.

571
00:19:19,095 --> 00:19:21,192
Monthly dividend stocks,

572
00:19:21,192 --> 00:19:23,201
REITs, rental property,

573
00:19:23,201 --> 00:19:26,607
covered call ETFs and income annuities.

574
00:19:26,787 --> 00:19:29,190
Higher yields with real trade offs.

575
00:19:29,370 --> 00:19:30,531
Now angle three.

576
00:19:30,711 --> 00:19:31,489
The playbook.

577
00:19:31,839 --> 00:19:34,760
The final five assets are about ownership

578
00:19:34,760 --> 00:19:36,398
and long term strategy,

579
00:19:36,398 --> 00:19:39,104
plus a plan to combine everything into

580
00:19:39,104 --> 00:19:40,814
reliable monthly income.

581
00:19:40,994 --> 00:19:43,070
Some of these take years to build,

582
00:19:43,070 --> 00:19:45,512
but they can become the strongest income

583
00:19:45,512 --> 00:19:46,427
sources of all.

584
00:19:46,607 --> 00:19:48,827
And unlike the safer assets in angle

585
00:19:48,827 --> 00:19:50,984
one, several of them can grow their

586
00:19:50,984 --> 00:19:52,155
payments over time,

587
00:19:52,155 --> 00:19:54,190
which is exactly what you need to

588
00:19:54,190 --> 00:19:56,285
keep up with inflation over a long

589
00:19:56,285 --> 00:19:56,963
retirement.

590
00:19:57,313 --> 00:19:58,672
Asset number eleven.

591
00:19:58,852 --> 00:20:01,093
A staggered dividend portfolio.

592
00:20:01,442 --> 00:20:04,331
Most dividend stocks and index funds pay

593
00:20:04,331 --> 00:20:05,053
quarterly.

594
00:20:05,233 --> 00:20:08,339
But because different companies pay in different

595
00:20:08,339 --> 00:20:11,574
months, a diversified portfolio can produce income

596
00:20:11,574 --> 00:20:12,803
almost every month.

597
00:20:13,152 --> 00:20:15,214
A broad dividend index fund,

598
00:20:15,214 --> 00:20:17,865
or simply a total market index fund,

599
00:20:17,865 --> 00:20:21,326
gives you dividends from hundreds of companies.

600
00:20:21,506 --> 00:20:24,211
Many investors also hold cash reserves to

601
00:20:24,211 --> 00:20:27,113
smooth out the months with smaller payments.

602
00:20:27,463 --> 00:20:29,913
Some retirees simply set up a fixed

603
00:20:29,913 --> 00:20:32,711
monthly withdrawal from their portfolio,

604
00:20:32,711 --> 00:20:35,231
combining dividends and small sales.

605
00:20:35,411 --> 00:20:37,285
That creates a steady paycheck,

606
00:20:37,285 --> 00:20:39,462
regardless of when dividends arrive.

607
00:20:39,812 --> 00:20:42,486
Focusing on dividend growth rather than high

608
00:20:42,486 --> 00:20:44,735
yield can help income rise over time.

609
00:20:44,915 --> 00:20:47,820
Companies with long records of raising dividends

610
00:20:47,820 --> 00:20:49,575
often have strong businesses,

611
00:20:49,575 --> 00:20:52,601
though past increases don't guarantee future ones.

612
00:20:52,950 --> 00:20:54,576
Here's an example of the math.

613
00:20:54,756 --> 00:20:57,978
A diversified stock index fund yielding around

614
00:20:57,978 --> 00:21:00,150
one and a half percent pays far

615
00:21:00,150 --> 00:21:02,742
less monthly income than a high yield

616
00:21:02,742 --> 00:21:03,092
fund.

617
00:21:03,272 --> 00:21:04,714
But over long periods,

618
00:21:04,714 --> 00:21:07,335
its growth has often been much stronger.

619
00:21:07,515 --> 00:21:10,285
Many retirees combine a modest dividend with

620
00:21:10,285 --> 00:21:11,544
small regular sales,

621
00:21:11,544 --> 00:21:14,629
creating a sustainable paycheck while keeping the

622
00:21:14,629 --> 00:21:16,014
growth engine running.

623
00:21:16,363 --> 00:21:17,234
Try this.

624
00:21:17,414 --> 00:21:19,978
Look at your brokerage's dividend calendar to

625
00:21:19,978 --> 00:21:21,687
see when your investments pay.

626
00:21:21,867 --> 00:21:24,254
You may already have more monthly income

627
00:21:24,254 --> 00:21:25,268
than you realize.

628
00:21:25,618 --> 00:21:26,872
Asset number twelve.

629
00:21:27,052 --> 00:21:28,399
A small business.

630
00:21:28,749 --> 00:21:31,339
A profitable small business can pay its

631
00:21:31,339 --> 00:21:32,534
owner every month.

632
00:21:32,714 --> 00:21:35,521
Some business owners eventually hire managers,

633
00:21:35,521 --> 00:21:37,901
so the business runs without them being

634
00:21:37,901 --> 00:21:38,877
there every day,

635
00:21:38,877 --> 00:21:41,074
turning it into a true income asset.

636
00:21:41,423 --> 00:21:43,751
Businesses with recurring revenue,

637
00:21:43,751 --> 00:21:47,791
like subscriptions, service contracts or maintenance plans,

638
00:21:47,791 --> 00:21:49,434
are especially valuable,

639
00:21:49,434 --> 00:21:52,720
because they produce predictable monthly income.

640
00:21:53,069 --> 00:21:54,977
But businesses take work,

641
00:21:54,977 --> 00:21:56,808
money and risk to build,

642
00:21:56,808 --> 00:21:57,876
and many fail.

643
00:21:58,056 --> 00:22:00,859
Most owners spend years working hard before

644
00:22:00,859 --> 00:22:03,141
income becomes even partly passive.

645
00:22:03,491 --> 00:22:06,468
Some people buy existing businesses with steady

646
00:22:06,468 --> 00:22:09,192
cash flow instead of starting from scratch.

647
00:22:09,372 --> 00:22:11,716
That still requires careful research,

648
00:22:11,716 --> 00:22:12,983
and often financing.

649
00:22:13,332 --> 00:22:15,490
The real value of a business often

650
00:22:15,490 --> 00:22:17,013
shows up when it's sold.

651
00:22:17,193 --> 00:22:19,868
Small businesses frequently sell for a few

652
00:22:19,868 --> 00:22:21,523
times their yearly profit.

653
00:22:21,703 --> 00:22:23,627
So a business that pays its owner

654
00:22:23,627 --> 00:22:25,960
fifty thousand dollars a year could also

655
00:22:25,960 --> 00:22:28,000
be worth one hundred to two hundred

656
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:29,633
thousand dollars to a buyer.

657
00:22:29,813 --> 00:22:31,404
That's a double benefit,

658
00:22:31,404 --> 00:22:33,990
monthly income now and a valuable asset

659
00:22:33,990 --> 00:22:34,387
later.

660
00:22:34,737 --> 00:22:35,631
Try this.

661
00:22:35,811 --> 00:22:38,434
Think about skills you already have that

662
00:22:38,434 --> 00:22:39,942
people pay for monthly,

663
00:22:39,942 --> 00:22:40,925
like lawn care,

664
00:22:40,925 --> 00:22:42,499
bookkeeping or tutoring.

665
00:22:42,679 --> 00:22:45,405
Recurring services can grow into a real

666
00:22:45,405 --> 00:22:46,034
business.

667
00:22:46,384 --> 00:22:47,766
Asset number thirteen.

668
00:22:47,946 --> 00:22:50,117
Royalties and digital products.

669
00:22:50,467 --> 00:22:52,399
Books, music, online courses,

670
00:22:52,399 --> 00:22:55,131
templates, stock photos and apps can earn

671
00:22:55,131 --> 00:22:57,329
money long after they're created.

672
00:22:57,509 --> 00:22:59,912
Many platforms pay creators monthly.

673
00:23:00,262 --> 00:23:02,283
The appeal is scalability.

674
00:23:02,463 --> 00:23:05,515
Selling one more digital copy costs almost

675
00:23:05,515 --> 00:23:06,097
nothing.

676
00:23:06,277 --> 00:23:09,214
A successful product can earn for years.

677
00:23:09,563 --> 00:23:12,150
The reality is that most creative products

678
00:23:12,150 --> 00:23:13,197
earn very little.

679
00:23:13,377 --> 00:23:15,371
It often takes many products,

680
00:23:15,371 --> 00:23:18,602
consistent marketing and years of effort before

681
00:23:18,602 --> 00:23:20,390
income becomes meaningful.

682
00:23:20,739 --> 00:23:22,771
Treat it like planting seeds.

683
00:23:22,951 --> 00:23:24,995
Each product is a small seed.

684
00:23:25,175 --> 00:23:26,323
Most stay small,

685
00:23:26,323 --> 00:23:28,475
but a few can grow into steady

686
00:23:28,475 --> 00:23:29,552
monthly income.

687
00:23:29,901 --> 00:23:32,154
Traditional royalties exist too.

688
00:23:32,334 --> 00:23:35,170
Authors earn royalties from book sales,

689
00:23:35,170 --> 00:23:37,860
musicians from streams and licensing,

690
00:23:37,860 --> 00:23:41,059
and some investors even buy existing royalty

691
00:23:41,059 --> 00:23:43,677
streams on specialized marketplaces.

692
00:23:43,857 --> 00:23:46,163
These can produce monthly payments,

693
00:23:46,163 --> 00:23:48,469
but they often decline over time as

694
00:23:48,469 --> 00:23:50,183
a song or book gets older,

695
00:23:50,183 --> 00:23:52,489
so they're best treated as a bonus,

696
00:23:52,489 --> 00:23:53,609
not a foundation.

697
00:23:53,959 --> 00:23:54,726
Try this.

698
00:23:54,906 --> 00:23:57,078
If you have knowledge people want,

699
00:23:57,078 --> 00:23:59,187
create one small digital product,

700
00:23:59,187 --> 00:24:00,785
like a guide or template,

701
00:24:00,785 --> 00:24:02,766
and see whether anyone buys it.

702
00:24:03,115 --> 00:24:04,474
Asset number fourteen.

703
00:24:04,654 --> 00:24:05,838
A paid off home.

704
00:24:06,188 --> 00:24:07,978
A paid off home doesn't send you

705
00:24:07,978 --> 00:24:10,159
a check, but it does something similar.

706
00:24:10,339 --> 00:24:12,371
It removes one of the biggest monthly

707
00:24:12,371 --> 00:24:13,799
expenses most people have.

708
00:24:13,979 --> 00:24:16,294
Every month without a mortgage payment is

709
00:24:16,294 --> 00:24:18,100
money that stays in your pocket.

710
00:24:18,450 --> 00:24:21,084
Economists call the value of living in

711
00:24:21,084 --> 00:24:23,025
a home you own imputed rent.

712
00:24:23,205 --> 00:24:25,422
You're effectively paying rent to yourself.

713
00:24:25,602 --> 00:24:27,651
For retirees, a paid off home can

714
00:24:27,651 --> 00:24:30,444
dramatically lower how much income they need.

715
00:24:30,793 --> 00:24:32,711
You still pay property taxes,

716
00:24:32,711 --> 00:24:34,430
